---
layout: article
title: Create an environment
description: Create an Appwrite project environment from the Console, as an empty environment or as a copy of the project, and switch between production and its environments.
difficulty: beginner
readtime: 4
---

An environment is created from its project. It lands in the project's organization and region, and it's ready to use as soon as it's created.

# Open the environment switcher {% #open-switcher %}

{% only_dark %}
![Environment switcher open in the project sidebar](/images/docs/environments/dark/switcher.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Environment switcher open in the project sidebar](/images/docs/environments/switcher.avif)
{% /only_light %}

1. Open your project in the [Appwrite Console](https://cloud.appwrite.io/console).
2. Click the environment switcher in the project sidebar, above **Overview**. It shows **Production** with a green dot.
3. Click **Create environment**.

# Name the environment {% #name %}

{% only_dark %}
![Create environment page with a name, an environment ID, and the Start from options](/images/docs/environments/dark/create.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create environment page with a name, an environment ID, and the Start from options](/images/docs/environments/create.avif)
{% /only_light %}

1. Enter a **Name**, such as `Staging`. The Console suggests one that isn't taken yet.
2. Check the **Environment ID**. It follows the name, so `Staging` gives `staging`. Apps pass this ID next to the project ID, so pick one that's easy to type. It can contain lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens, starts with a letter or a number, and can't be changed later. Leave it blank to generate a random ID.

# Choose where to start {% #start-from %}

Under **Start from**, choose one of:

- **Empty environment.** Nothing is copied. The environment starts like a new project.
- **Clone of the project.** The project's structure and settings are copied, and data only where you tick it. Choose what to copy under **What to copy**. [Learn what a copy includes](/docs/advanced/environments/copy).

{% only_dark %}
![What to copy list with structure selected for every group and data unticked](/images/docs/environments/dark/copy.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![What to copy list with structure selected for every group and data unticked](/images/docs/environments/copy.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Click **Create environment**. The Console opens the new environment.

If you cloned the project, a card in the bottom-right corner shows the copy's progress. The environment is usable while the copy runs, and its pages refresh when the copy finishes.

# Work inside an environment {% #inside %}

{% only_dark %}
![Purple environment banner above a project page, with a Switch to production button](/images/docs/environments/dark/banner.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Purple environment banner above a project page, with a Switch to production button](/images/docs/environments/banner.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Inside an environment, a purple banner at the top of every project page names the environment, and the environment switcher shows its name with a purple dot. Every page of the project, from Databases to Sites, now shows and changes the environment's resources instead of production's.

The project's **Get started** page shows the endpoint, project ID, and environment ID to [connect an app to the environment](/docs/advanced/environments/connect).

# Switch environments {% #switch %}

Open the environment switcher and pick **Production** or another environment. The **Switch to production** button on the banner goes straight back to production.

The Console keeps you on the same project URL and remembers the environment you picked for each project in the current browser tab. A different tab can work in a different environment of the same project.

# Create environments with the API {% #api %}

Environments can also be created with an API key that has the `project.write` scope. Send a `POST` request to `/v1/project/environments` with the project's ID in the `X-Appwrite-Project` header:

```bash
curl -X POST https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/project/environments \
    -H "X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>" \
    -H "X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{
        "environmentId": "staging",
        "name": "Staging",
        "resources": ["database", "table", "column", "index", "bucket", "function", "environment-variable"]
    }'
```

Leave out `resources` to create an empty environment. The same route lists environments with `GET`, and `/v1/project/environments/<ENVIRONMENT_ID>` gets, renames (`PATCH` with a new `name`), or deletes one.

